open scoped TensorProduct

/-!
# Descent of a finitely presented algebra and module to a subring of finite type over `ℤ`

Proof of Stacks 00R1. Let `R → S` be of finite presentation and let `M` be a finitely presented
`S`-module. Write `S = R[x_1, …, x_n]/(f_1, …, f_m)` and write `M` as the cokernel of a `t × s`
matrix `v` over `S`. Let `R₀ ⊆ R` be the subring generated by the coefficients of the `f_j`
together with the coefficients of a chosen polynomial lift of each entry of `v`; it is generated
by a finite set, hence of finite type over `ℤ`. Then `S₀ = R₀[x_1, …, x_n]/(f_1, …, f_m)` is of
finite presentation over `R₀` with `R ⊗_{R₀} S₀ = S`, the columns of `v` lift to `S₀^t`, and
`M₀ = S₀^t/(lifted columns)` is a finite `S₀`-module with `S ⊗_{S₀} M₀ = M`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.FinitePresentation.ModuleDescent.solution`: Stacks 00R1.

## Implementation notes

The two halves of the descent are two lemmas.

The algebra half is
`Stacks.FinitePresentation.ModuleDescent.exists_algebra_isPushout_modelOfHasCoeffs`: the model `S₀`
of a presentation over a ring `R₀` containing its coefficients carries an `R₀`-algebra map to `S`,
sending the class of a polynomial `y` to `aeval P.val y`, and along it `S` is the base change of
`S₀`. Mathlib has the isomorphism `R ⊗_{R₀} S₀ ≃ S` (`Algebra.Presentation.ModelOfHasCoeffs` and
`Algebra.Presentation.tensorModelOfHasCoeffsEquiv`) but neither that algebra structure nor the
`Algebra.IsPushout` form of the isomorphism, which is what the descent conditions are phrased
with. The coefficients are taken in the subring generated by the enlarged coefficient set rather
than in `Algebra.Presentation.Core`, which sees only the coefficients of the relations and is in
general too small to carry the module.

The module half is `Stacks.FinitePresentation.ModuleDescent.isBaseChange_of_surjective`: base
change along `A → B` preserves cokernels, in the form that if `u : P₀ → P` exhibits `P` as the base
change of `P₀`, and `π₀ : P₀ → M₀`, `π : P → M` are surjective with
`ker π ≤ span_B (u '' ker π₀)`, then any `f : M₀ → M` with `f ∘ π₀ = π ∘ u` exhibits `M` as the
base change of `M₀`. This is the
statement Mathlib is missing: it has right exactness of the tensor product but not its
`IsBaseChange` form, and `IsBaseChange` is again what the descent conditions are phrased with.

Both the definition of `M₀` and the hypothesis of that lemma come from one computation,
`Stacks.FinitePresentation.ModuleDescent.span_image_span_range`: lifts `w₀` of a generating family
`v` generate the same submodule after base change, `span_B (u '' span_A (range w₀)) = span_B (range
v)`. Read as `≤` it says that the lifted relations still annihilate `M`, so that `M₀ =
S₀^t/span(w₀)` maps to `M`; read as `≥` it is the hypothesis of `isBaseChange_of_surjective`.

The `AddCommGroup` structure on the auxiliary module `Q` supplied by
`IsBaseChange.of_lift_unique` is only an `AddCommMonoid`, so it is upgraded by
`Module.addCommMonoidToAddCommGroup` before `Submodule.liftQ` is used on it.

## References

* [StacksProject 00R1](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00R1)
-/

open TensorProduct

namespace Stacks.FinitePresentation.ModuleDescent



variable {R S ι σ : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [Algebra R S]

/-- **A linear map killing the kernel of a surjection factors through it.** Stated as an equation
of composites, which is the form `LinearMap.congr_fun` turns into the pointwise one. -/
public theorem exists_comp_eq_of_ker_le_ker {B : Type*} [CommRing B] {P M Q : Type*}
    [AddCommGroup P] [Module B P] [AddCommGroup M] [Module B M] [AddCommGroup Q] [Module B Q]
    (π : P →ₗ[B] M) (hπ : Function.Surjective π) (G : P →ₗ[B] Q)
    (h : LinearMap.ker π ≤ LinearMap.ker G) : ∃ g' : M →ₗ[B] Q, g' ∘ₗ π = G :=
  ⟨(LinearMap.ker π).liftQ G h ∘ₗ (LinearMap.quotKerEquivOfSurjective π hπ).symm.toLinearMap,
    LinearMap.ext fun _ => by simp⟩

/-- **Base change preserves cokernels.** Let `u : P₀ →ₗ[A] P` exhibit `P` as the base change of
`P₀` along `A → B`, let `π₀ : P₀ →ₗ[A] M₀` and `π : P →ₗ[B] M` be surjective, and let
`f : M₀ →ₗ[A] M` satisfy `f ∘ π₀ = π ∘ u`. If the kernel of `π` is generated over `B` by the
image under `u` of the kernel of `π₀`, then `f` exhibits `M` as the base change of `M₀`. -/
private theorem isBaseChange_of_surjective
    {A B : Type*} [CommRing A] [CommRing B] [Algebra A B]
    {P₀ M₀ P M : Type*}
    [AddCommGroup P₀] [Module A P₀] [AddCommGroup M₀] [Module A M₀]
    [AddCommGroup P] [Module A P] [Module B P] [IsScalarTower A B P]
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] [Module B M] [IsScalarTower A B M]
    {u : P₀ →ₗ[A] P} (hu : IsBaseChange B u)
    {π₀ : P₀ →ₗ[A] M₀} (hπ₀ : Function.Surjective π₀)
    {π : P →ₗ[B] M} (hπ : Function.Surjective π)
    {f : M₀ →ₗ[A] M} (hcomm : ∀ x, f (π₀ x) = π (u x))
    (hker : LinearMap.ker π ≤ Submodule.span B (u '' LinearMap.ker π₀)) :
    IsBaseChange B f := by
  apply IsBaseChange.of_lift_unique
  intro Q _ _ _ _ g
  set G := hu.lift (g ∘ₗ π₀)
  have hGu : ∀ x, G (u x) = g (π₀ x) := fun x => hu.lift_eq _ x
  have hkerG : LinearMap.ker π ≤ LinearMap.ker G := by
    refine hker.trans ?_
    rw [Submodule.span_le]
    rintro _ ⟨y, hy, rfl⟩
    simp only [SetLike.mem_coe, LinearMap.mem_ker, hGu]
    rw [LinearMap.mem_ker.1 hy, map_zero]
  let _ : AddCommGroup Q := Module.addCommMonoidToAddCommGroup B
  obtain ⟨g', hg'comp⟩ := exists_comp_eq_of_ker_le_ker π hπ G hkerG
  have hg'π : ∀ x, g' (π x) = G x := LinearMap.congr_fun hg'comp
  refine ⟨g', ?_, ?_⟩
  · ext m₀
    obtain ⟨x, rfl⟩ := hπ₀ m₀
    change g' (f (π₀ x)) = g (π₀ x)
    rw [hcomm, hg'π, hGu]
  · intro g'' hg''
    have key : g'' ∘ₗ π = g' ∘ₗ π := by
      refine hu.algHom_ext _ _ fun x => ?_
      change g'' (π (u x)) = g' (π (u x))
      rw [hg'π, hGu, ← hcomm]
      exact LinearMap.congr_fun hg'' (π₀ x)
    ext m
    obtain ⟨x, rfl⟩ := hπ m
    exact LinearMap.congr_fun key x

/-- **Lifts of a generating family generate after base change.** If `u : P₀ →ₗ[A] P` sends a
family `w₀` to a family `v`, then the `B`-span of the image of the `A`-span of `w₀` is the
`B`-span of `v`. -/
private theorem span_image_span_range {A B : Type*} [CommRing A] [CommRing B] [Algebra A B]
    {P₀ P κ : Type*} [AddCommGroup P₀] [Module A P₀] [AddCommGroup P] [Module A P] [Module B P]
    [IsScalarTower A B P] (u : P₀ →ₗ[A] P) {w₀ : κ → P₀} {v : κ → P} (h : ∀ i, u (w₀ i) = v i) :
    Submodule.span B (u '' Submodule.span A (Set.range w₀)) = Submodule.span B (Set.range v) := by
  rw [← Submodule.map_coe, Submodule.map_span, ← Set.range_comp,
    show u ∘ w₀ = v from funext h, Submodule.span_span_of_tower]

/-- **The model of a presentation over a subring of coefficients presents `S` as a base change.**
If `R₀` contains the coefficients of the relations of a presentation `P` of `S` over `R`, then
`S₀ = P.ModelOfHasCoeffs R₀` carries an `R₀`-algebra map to `S`, sending the class of a
polynomial `y` to `aeval P.val y`, and `S` is the base change of `S₀` along `R₀ → R`. -/
private theorem exists_algebra_isPushout_modelOfHasCoeffs (P : Algebra.Presentation R S ι σ)
    (R₀ : Type*) [CommRing R₀] [Algebra R₀ R] [Algebra R₀ S] [IsScalarTower R₀ R S]
    [P.HasCoeffs R₀] :
    ∃ (_ : Algebra (P.ModelOfHasCoeffs R₀) S) (_ : IsScalarTower R₀ (P.ModelOfHasCoeffs R₀) S),
      Algebra.IsPushout R₀ R (P.ModelOfHasCoeffs R₀) S ∧
        ∀ y : MvPolynomial ι R₀, algebraMap (P.ModelOfHasCoeffs R₀) S (Ideal.Quotient.mk _ y) =
          MvPolynomial.aeval P.val y := by
  let toS : P.ModelOfHasCoeffs R₀ →ₐ[R₀] S :=
    ((P.tensorModelOfHasCoeffsHom R₀).restrictScalars R₀).comp Algebra.TensorProduct.includeRight
  let _ : Algebra (P.ModelOfHasCoeffs R₀) S := toS.toAlgebra
  have htower : IsScalarTower R₀ (P.ModelOfHasCoeffs R₀) S :=
    IsScalarTower.of_algebraMap_eq fun x => (toS.commutes x).symm
  have key : ∀ y : MvPolynomial ι R₀,
      P.tensorModelOfHasCoeffsEquiv R₀ (1 ⊗ₜ[R₀] Ideal.Quotient.mk _ y) =
        MvPolynomial.aeval P.val y := fun y => by
    rw [P.tensorModelOfHasCoeffsEquiv_tmul, map_one, one_mul]
  have halg : ∀ y : MvPolynomial ι R₀,
      algebraMap (P.ModelOfHasCoeffs R₀) S (Ideal.Quotient.mk _ y) =
        MvPolynomial.aeval P.val y := key
  refine ⟨_, htower, ⟨IsBaseChange.of_equiv (P.tensorModelOfHasCoeffsEquiv R₀).toLinearEquiv
    fun x => ?_⟩, halg⟩
  obtain ⟨y, rfl⟩ := Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective x
  exact (key y).trans (halg y).symm

/-- An element of `S` whose chosen polynomial lift has all coefficients in `R₀` comes from the
model of the presentation over `R₀`. -/
private theorem exists_algebraMap_eq_of_coeffs_subset_range (P : Algebra.Presentation R S ι σ)
    (R₀ : Type*) [CommRing R₀] [Algebra R₀ R] [Algebra R₀ S] [IsScalarTower R₀ R S]
    [P.HasCoeffs R₀] [Algebra (P.ModelOfHasCoeffs R₀) S]
    (halg : ∀ y : MvPolynomial ι R₀, algebraMap (P.ModelOfHasCoeffs R₀) S (Ideal.Quotient.mk _ y) =
      MvPolynomial.aeval P.val y)
    {x : S} (hx : ((P.σ x).coeffs : Set R) ⊆ Set.range (algebraMap R₀ R)) :
    ∃ y : P.ModelOfHasCoeffs R₀, algebraMap (P.ModelOfHasCoeffs R₀) S y = x := by
  obtain ⟨b, hb⟩ := MvPolynomial.mem_range_map_iff_coeffs_subset.2 hx
  exact ⟨Ideal.Quotient.mk _ b, by
    rw [halg, ← MvPolynomial.aeval_map_algebraMap R, hb, P.aeval_val_σ]⟩

/-- A subring generated by a finite subset is of finite type over `ℤ`. -/
private theorem finiteType_int_closure {T : Set R} (hT : T.Finite) :
    Algebra.FiniteType ℤ ↥(Subring.closure T) :=
  (Algebra.FiniteType.adjoin_of_finite hT).equiv (Subring.closureEquivAdjoinInt T).symm

/-- **Stacks 00R1.** A finitely presented `R`-algebra `S` and a finitely presented `S`-module `M`
descend to a subring `R₀ ⊆ R` of finite type over `ℤ`: there are an `R₀`-algebra `S₀` of finite
type and a finite `S₀`-module `M₀` with `S = R ⊗_{R₀} S₀` and `M = S ⊗_{S₀} M₀`. -/
public theorem solution
    {R S : Type u} [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [Algebra R S] [Algebra.FinitePresentation R S]
    (M : Type v) [AddCommGroup M] [Module S M] [Module.FinitePresentation S M] :
    ∃ (R₀ : Subring R) (S₀ M₀ : Type u) (_ : CommRing S₀) (_ : Algebra R₀ S₀)
      (_ : Algebra S₀ S) (_ : IsScalarTower R₀ S₀ S) (_ : AddCommGroup M₀)
      (_ : Module S₀ M₀) (_ : Module S₀ M) (_ : IsScalarTower S₀ S M) (f : M₀ →ₗ[S₀] M),
      Algebra.FiniteType ℤ R₀ ∧ Algebra.FiniteType R₀ S₀ ∧ Module.Finite S₀ M₀ ∧
        Algebra.IsPushout R₀ R S₀ S ∧ IsBaseChange S f := by
  classical
  -- a finite presentation `S = R[x_1, …, x_n]/(f_1, …, f_m)`
  set P := Algebra.Presentation.ofFinitePresentation R S with hP
  -- `M` as the cokernel of a `t × s` matrix `v` over `S`
  obtain ⟨t, K, e, hK⟩ := Module.FinitePresentation.exists_fin S M
  obtain ⟨s, v, hv⟩ := Submodule.fg_iff_exists_fin_generating_family.1 hK
  set π : (Fin t → S) →ₗ[S] M := e.symm.toLinearMap ∘ₗ K.mkQ with hπdef
  have hπsurj : Function.Surjective π := e.symm.surjective.comp K.mkQ_surjective
  have hπker : LinearMap.ker π = Submodule.span S (Set.range v) := by
    rw [hπdef, LinearMap.ker_comp, LinearEquiv.ker, Submodule.comap_bot, Submodule.ker_mkQ, hv]
  -- the coefficients to be kept: those of the `f_j`, and those of the chosen polynomial lift of
  -- every entry of `v`
  set cf : Set R :=
    P.coeffs ∪ ⋃ p : Fin s × Fin t, ((P.σ (v p.1 p.2)).coeffs : Set R) with hcfdef
  have hcf : cf.Finite :=
    P.finite_coeffs.union (Set.finite_iUnion fun _ => Finset.finite_toSet _)
  set R₀ : Subring R := Subring.closure cf with hR₀def
  have hmem : ∀ x ∈ cf, x ∈ R₀ := fun x hx => Subring.subset_closure hx
  have : P.HasCoeffs ↥R₀ := ⟨fun x hx => ⟨⟨x, hmem x (Or.inl hx)⟩, rfl⟩⟩
  set S₀ : Type u := P.ModelOfHasCoeffs ↥R₀ with hS₀def
  obtain ⟨_, _, hpush, halg⟩ := exists_algebra_isPushout_modelOfHasCoeffs P ↥R₀
  -- `M` as an `S₀`-module, by restriction of scalars along `S₀ → S`
  let _ : Module S₀ M := Module.compHom M (algebraMap S₀ S)
  have : IsScalarTower S₀ S M := IsScalarTower.of_algebraMap_smul fun _ _ => rfl
  -- `S^t` is the base change of `S₀^t`
  set u : (Fin t → S₀) →ₗ[S₀] (Fin t → S) := (Algebra.linearMap S₀ S).compLeft (Fin t) with hudef
  have hu : IsBaseChange S u := IsBaseChange.finitePow (Fin t) (IsBaseChange.linearMap S₀ S)
  -- the columns of `v` lift to `S₀^t`
  choose w₀ hw₀ using fun (j : Fin s) (i : Fin t) =>
    exists_algebraMap_eq_of_coeffs_subset_range P ↥R₀ halg (x := v j i)
      fun c hc => ⟨⟨c, hmem c (Or.inr (Set.mem_iUnion.2 ⟨(j, i), hc⟩))⟩, rfl⟩
  set N₀ : Submodule S₀ (Fin t → S₀) := Submodule.span S₀ (Set.range w₀) with hN₀def
  have hspan : Submodule.span S (u '' (N₀ : Set (Fin t → S₀))) = LinearMap.ker π := by
    rw [hN₀def, span_image_span_range u fun j => funext (hw₀ j), hπker]
  have hN₀le : N₀ ≤ LinearMap.ker ((π.restrictScalars S₀) ∘ₗ u) := fun n hn => by
    have h : u n ∈ LinearMap.ker π := hspan ▸ Submodule.subset_span ⟨n, hn, rfl⟩
    simpa [LinearMap.mem_ker] using h
  refine ⟨R₀, S₀, (Fin t → S₀) ⧸ N₀, inferInstance, inferInstance, inferInstance, inferInstance,
    inferInstance, inferInstance, inferInstance, inferInstance,
    N₀.liftQ ((π.restrictScalars S₀) ∘ₗ u) hN₀le, finiteType_int_closure hcf, inferInstance,
    Module.Finite.of_surjective N₀.mkQ N₀.mkQ_surjective, hpush,
    isBaseChange_of_surjective hu N₀.mkQ_surjective hπsurj (fun x => rfl) ?_⟩
  rw [Submodule.ker_mkQ]
  exact hspan.ge

end Stacks.FinitePresentation.ModuleDescent

/-!
# Descent of a finitely presented algebra and module to a subring of finite type over `ℤ`

Let `R → S` be a ring map of finite presentation and let `M` be a finitely presented
`S`-module. Then the whole datum `(R → S, M)` is a base change of a datum over a subring of `R`
of finite type over `ℤ`: there are a subring `R₀ ⊆ R` of finite type over `ℤ`, an `R₀`-algebra
`S₀` of finite type and a finite `S₀`-module `M₀` such that the given maps `S₀ → S` and
`M₀ → M` exhibit `S = R ⊗_{R₀} S₀` and `M = S ⊗_{S₀} M₀`.

Writing `S` as `R[x_1, …, x_n]/(f_1, …, f_m)` and `M` as the cokernel of a matrix
`A ∈ Mat(t × s, S)`, one takes for `R₀` the subring generated by the coefficients of the `f_j`
together with those of a lift of `A` to `R[x_1, …, x_n]`, for `S₀` the algebra
`R₀[x_1, …, x_n]/(f_1, …, f_m)` and for `M₀` the cokernel of `A` over `S₀`.

## Main results

* `Algebra.exists_subring_finiteType_int_isPushout_isBaseChange`: a ring map of finite
  presentation together with a finitely presented module over its target is a base change of
  such a datum over a subring of the source of finite type over `ℤ`.

## Implementation notes

The source states this as a directed colimit: a directed set `Λ`, a system of ring maps
`R_λ → S_λ` with `S_λ`-modules `M_λ`, with colimit `(R → S, M)`, each `R_λ` of finite type over
`ℤ`, each `S_λ` of finite type over `R_λ`, each `M_λ` finite over `S_λ`, and the transition maps
cartesian: `S_λ ⊗_{R_λ} R_μ ≅ S_μ` and `M_λ ⊗_{S_λ} S_μ ≅ M_μ` for `λ ≤ μ`. That form and the
descent stated here are equivalent, and the descent is the sharper of the two.

From the descent one builds the system by taking for `Λ` the set of subrings of `R` of finite
type over `ℤ` containing `R₀`, ordered by inclusion — directed, with union `R`, since every
finite subset of `R` generates such a subring — and setting `R_λ = λ`, `S_λ = R_λ ⊗_{R₀} S₀` and
`M_λ = S_λ ⊗_{S₀} M₀`. The cartesian conditions then hold by associativity of base change, the
colimits are `R`, `R ⊗_{R₀} S₀ = S` and `S ⊗_{S₀} M₀ = M`, and finiteness in each row is
inherited from `R₀ → S₀` and `M₀`. Conversely, a system as in the source yields the descent at
any `λ`: for `R₀` the image of `R_λ` in `R`, which is again of finite type over `ℤ`, and
`S₀ = R₀ ⊗_{R_λ} S_λ`, `M₀ = S₀ ⊗_{S_λ} M_λ`. The descent is sharper in that `R₀` is a subring
of `R`, whereas the source's `R_λ → R` need not be injective, and in that `S₀` and `M₀` are
produced in the universe of `R` while `M` is arbitrary.

`S = R ⊗_{R₀} S₀` is `Algebra.IsPushout R₀ R S₀ S`, which unfolds to
`IsBaseChange R (IsScalarTower.toAlgHom R₀ S₀ S).toLinearMap`: the given algebra
map `S₀ → S` induces a bijection `R ⊗_{R₀} S₀ → S`. Likewise `M = S ⊗_{S₀} M₀` is
`IsBaseChange S f` for the given `f : M₀ →ₗ[S₀] M`. Restriction of scalars along
`S₀ → S` is not an instance in Mathlib, so the `S₀`-module structure on `M` used to state `f`
is part of the existential; `IsScalarTower S₀ S M` determines it uniquely as the restriction,
so nothing is chosen.

The source's finiteness conditions (3) and (4) are kept verbatim as `Algebra.FiniteType R₀ S₀`
and `Module.Finite S₀ M₀`. They are equivalent to the corresponding finite presentation
statements: `R₀` is Noetherian (`Algebra.FiniteType.isNoetherianRing`), hence so is `S₀`, so
`Algebra.FinitePresentation.of_finiteType` and `Module.finitePresentation_of_finite` upgrade them
for free.

The source's closing identity `M = M_λ ⊗_{S_λ} S = M_λ ⊗_{R_λ} R` is not restated: the first
equality is `IsBaseChange S f`, and the second follows from it and
`Algebra.IsPushout R₀ R S₀ S` by cancelling the base change along `R₀ → S₀`.

Faithfulness. (1) No hypothesis is dead: both are in fact equivalent to the conclusion, since
`S₀` is of finite presentation over the Noetherian `R₀` and `M₀` over `S₀`, and finite
presentation of an algebra and of a module is stable under base change
(`Algebra.FinitePresentation.baseChange`, `FinitePresentation.of_isBaseChange`). (2) The
scalars and bases are the source's: `R₀` is a subring of the source's `R`, `S₀` is an
`R₀`-algebra and `M₀` an `S₀`-module, `S` is descended along `R₀ → R` and `M` along `S₀ → S`,
matching `S_λ ⊗_{R_λ} R` and `M_λ ⊗_{S_λ} S`. (3) The canonical maps are pinned: the claim is
that the *given* maps `S₀ → S` and `f : M₀ → M` induce bijections `R ⊗_{R₀} S₀ → S` and
`S ⊗_{S₀} M₀ → M`, not that some isomorphism exists. (4) The instantiation is not degenerate:
for `R = ℚ`, `S = ℚ[y]/(y² - 2)` and `M = S ⊕ S/(y)` the conclusion holds with `R₀ = ℤ`,
`S₀ = ℤ[y]/(y² - 2)` and `M₀ = S₀ ⊕ S₀/(y)`, and it cannot be met by the trivial choice
`R₀ = R`, since `ℚ` is not of finite type over `ℤ`.

